Condition system: SIGNAL of a MAKE-CONDITION'd condition doesn't match handlers
(CLOS / kernel condition-class duality).  + WARN doesn't establish MUFFLE-WARNING.

== RESOLVED (Slice 88) ==
Fixed by bridging the two hierarchies in subclassp (sources/kernel.c): a class
designator now matches a superclass either by object identity OR — across the
kernel-class / CLOS-%cls split — by having the same non-NIL class NAME
(class_designator_name handles both representations; cached sym_class_name = the
%cls 'NAME slot).  CL class names are unique, so name equality is exactly the
"same logical class" relation; the symbolp guard rejects any stray non-class.
WARN is re-implemented in Lisp (prelude) wrapping the signal in a MUFFLE-WARNING
restart-case, which now works because the warning (a make-condition'd CLOS
instance) matches the WARNING handler.

Verified: (signal (make-condition 'simple-warning ...)) is caught by a WARNING
handler; handler-case/handler-bind catch make-condition'd conditions; a user
DEFINE-CONDITION subclass of a kernel condition is caught by a kernel-class
handler; WARN + (invoke-restart 'muffle-warning) muffles (output empty); no
regression in typep/subtypep/CLOS/defmethod/error/bignum or other SACLA files.

NB: under `-on-error appease` the must-condition skip count rose 3 -> 6 — an
ARTIFACT, not a regression: the load-recovery handler-case now correctly catches
deliberately-(signal)'d ERROR conditions that previously slipped past it (the
duality), so those deftest forms are "skipped" by the wrapper.  In a normal run
(no wrapper) they pass — verified F1 (signal 'simple-error)->NIL and F36
(handler-bind type-error + signal type-error).  The remaining genuine skip is one
"check_number: expected NUMBER got NULL" form.

(Original analysis below.)

Context: after the restart-system work (Slices 84-87) the SACLA must-condition file
went from 58 skipped forms to 3.  The remaining 3, and a class of test FAILURES
(forms that now run but return the wrong value), all trace to one root cause.

== Symptom ==
A condition built by MAKE-CONDITION and then SIGNALed is NOT seen by a matching
HANDLER-BIND / HANDLER-CASE handler — not even for the exact class:

    (let ((r nil))
      (handler-bind ((simple-warning (lambda (c) (setq r t))))
        (signal (make-condition 'simple-warning :format-control "x")))
      r)                                  ; => NIL   (handler never ran)

But conditions built the kernel way DO match:

    (handler-case (error "x")          (error (c) :ok))     ; => :OK
    (handler-case (signal "x")         (condition () :ok))  ; => :OK  (restart tests rely on this)

== Root cause ==
MAKE-CONDITION is overridden in the prelude (bootstrap/prelude.lisp ~4273,
"prefer CLOS class lookup, fall back to kernel"): for a class that is %is-cls it
calls (make-instance cls ...), producing a CLOS instance.  SIGNAL's handler
matching (signal_condition in sources/conditions.c) tests
subclassp(class_of(cond), handler-type-class), where the handler type is resolved
to a KERNEL condition class (e.g. WARNING_Class).  subclassp does not bridge the
CLOS instance's class to the kernel condition classes, so the match fails.
(error "x") / (signal "x") build kernel conditions via build_condition ->
make_simple_condition, whose class IS a kernel condition class, so subclassp via
direct_superclasses (wired in kernel_objects.c ~670-694) succeeds.

== The 3 remaining must-condition skips ==
  - 2x  "INVOKE-RESTART: no active restart named MUFFLE-WARNING" — WARN
        (kernel_WARN, conditions.c) signals a warning but does NOT establish a
        MUFFLE-WARNING restart, so a handler that (invoke-restart 'muffle-warning)
        errors.  CLHS requires WARN to bind that restart around the signal.
  - 1x  "check_number: expected NUMBER, got NULL" — a separate, unexamined form.

== Attempts (reverted) ==
  - Lisp WARN wrapping (signal cond) in (restart-case ... (muffle-warning () nil)):
    establishes the restart correctly, BUT builds the warning with MAKE-CONDITION
    -> CLOS instance -> the warning handler doesn't match (same root cause), so
    the warning is not muffled.  Reverted (it is worse than the kernel WARN for
    handler matching).
  - subclassp fall-through to direct_superclasses when a clos_class_p class has an
    empty CPL (kernel.c): did NOT fix the symptom (so class_of of the
    make-condition'd object is not simply the kernel class, or the CPL is
    non-empty-but-wrong) and had no observable benefit; reverted.

== Fix options (pick one) ==
  (a) Make MAKE-CONDITION use the KERNEL constructor for kernel built-in condition
      classes (simple-warning, simple-error, …) and CLOS make-instance only for
      user DEFINE-CONDITION classes — so all standard conditions are kernel
      conditions that subclassp matches.  Cleanest if "is this a kernel condition
      class" is cheaply testable.
  (b) Bridge CLOS condition instances to the kernel hierarchy in subclassp /
      class_of so a CLOS instance of (a subclass of) a kernel condition class
      matches kernel-class handler types.  Investigate what class_of returns for a
      (make-instance SIMPLE_WARNING_Class) and whether its CPL includes the kernel
      condition class objects.
  (c) Resolve HANDLER-BIND / HANDLER-CASE handler types through the SAME path
      MAKE-CONDITION uses (CLOS find-class) so both sides are CLOS, and make
      subclassp consistent across both.

Once (a)/(b)/(c) lands, WARN can be the Lisp restart-case wrapper and the muffle
tests pass, taking must-condition to ~1 skip (the check_number form).
